Subject: [SUMMARY] DEC3000-M400: Y2k and firmware.
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96079040315820

Hi Tru64ers

My questions were about Y2k compliance of the DEC3000-M400 and
related to that whether a firmware upgrade was really needed
to upgrade to OS version 4.0E.

I posted a preliminary summary earlier but now I've got some more 
information.

On the subject of Y2k compliance:

Rick Rosa of Compaq Australia pointed me to:

   http://ww1.digital.com/year2000/position.asp

It says there:

   All AlphaServer systems and Alpha based workstations 
   running Year 2000 Ready versions of the operating systems 
   are Year 2000 Ready. 

So I take that to mean our DEC 3000s are compliant and since
they're not mentioned in the list of exceptions I assume the
firmware is compliant as well.

I got a few queries about this quote in my first summary:

> There are a few older ethernet controllers that won't be Y2K compliant.
> AFAIK, that's the only hardware-related issue.

So I asked Alan for some more information.  He said:

>  I no longer have access to the internal Y2K qual databases.
> I searched the one on the web site (which is supposed to match,
> but because there are two vastly different groups managing them, they don't)
> and the DE425 isn't listed. That's the one that I remember having problems.
> When I asked, I was told that there are registers on the board that aren't
> large enough to handle the y2k time/date stamp from the network packets.


On the subject of the firmware:

Most replies recommended upgrading the firmware but a few said
they were running 4.0D or E successfully with older firmware.
I decided to go ahead and upgrade one machine to 4.0E without
upgrading the firmware.

That was last Sunday and it's been running without a hitch since
with scores of logins starting about an hour after I finished the
upgrade - on Sunday morning! (This place never sleeps).


Regards
Ian

