Dear all Du Folk

	A plethora of responses - all pretty much spot on the mark.
	My running kernel would not find the KZPBA-CA device - so no matter
how tricky I tried to be with a recompile of MAKEDEV, no joy.
	Booting from genvmunix then running doconfig was the answer - I am
somewhat embarrassed by the simplicity of the solution.
	Gyula Szokoly also had an interesting response regarding manual
addition to /sys/conf/HOSTNAME.
	His response is:

	 			An other possibility is that even genvmunix
does not support the new
			card. This was true at one point for some
turbochannel cards (like mine
			in a 3000/600). In that case I had to do it by hand.
I had to insert by
			hand the following in /sys/conf/HOSTNAME (replace
hostname with the
			real one):

			bus             tcds1      at tc0       slot  2
vector    tcdsintr
			controller      scsi2      at tcds1     slot  0
			controller      scsi3      at tcds1     slot  1

		  	Than I did a '/sys/conf/config HOSTNAME', followed
by a 'make' in
			/sys/HOSTNAME, to build a new kernel. Try genvmunix
first
			('boot -fi "genvmunix").


My thanks to him and to the MANY and rapid responses from everybody else. 
This mailing list has never let me down and is an excellent reference
library.


ORIGINAL QUESTION:
I have an Alphaserver800 with latest firmware, running DU4.0D. I
have installed a KZPBA-CA SCSI controller for this machine. At SRM level I
can see this device no problem. However when I boot into TRU64 Unix I am
beating my brow  trying to see the device. 
	There is a DLT 35-70GB tape drive attached to the end of the SCSI
controller and I have run the "eeromcfg" utility from alphabios and this
appears to be okay - SCSI termination options set to auto-terminate at
controller. Physical termination on tape drive.
	I have recompiled the kernel with "doconfig -a", even tried a
MAKEDEV of "TMS".
	Still I cannot see the tape drive , let along the scsi controller. 
	I feel I am missing doing something very obvious.
	Any opinions / advice gratefully received.


